From e08a003c61258f2638259001520c8b839f21e5b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 20:58:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Use batch-norm moving averages at inference in the MuNet
 decoder

The multi-stage decoder in PoseMultiNet (MuNet/DLCRNet, used when
pose_cfg.yaml sets stride < 8 together with multi_stage: true) wraps its
five decoder_parallel_* convolutions in

    slim.arg_scope([slim.conv2d], normalizer_fn=slim.layers.batch_norm, ...)

without any normalizer_params.  tf_slim's batch_norm defaults to
is_training=True, so those layers normalise with the *current batch's*
statistics in every graph DeepLabCut builds -- including the inference
graphs built by BasePoseNet.test() and BasePoseNet.inference().  Dumping
the graph confirms it: under decoder_filters all five FusedBatchNormV3
nodes carry is_training=True, while the 53 backbone nodes correctly carry
is_training=False, since extract_features passes is_training=False to
resnet_v1.  The inference graph even registers ten dangling moving-average
update ops in tf.GraphKeys.UPDATE_OPS that nothing ever runs.

Consequence: the prediction for a given frame depends on which other
frames happen to share its batch.  Measured on a MuNet (resnet_50,
stride 4, multi_stage) trained for 10k iterations, running
deeplabcut.analyze_videos over the same 16-frame video:

    batchsize 1 vs batchsize 8   mean 0.323 px, p95 0.929 px, max 2.665 px

Holding the batch size at 4 and changing only which images accompany a
fixed probe image moves that image's own keypoints by up to 0.30 px for
natural companions and up to 8.9 px when the companions are blank frames.
Because evaluate_network forces batch_size = 1 while analyze_videos
defaults to batch_size = 8, the reported evaluation error and the
keypoints written out for a video were not even produced by the same
normalisation.

Fix: thread an is_training flag from the three graph-building entry points
(train / test / inference) down to the decoder's arg_scope and pass it to
batch_norm through normalizer_params.  train() keeps is_training=True, so
training is unchanged -- the training graph still contains the same five
is_training=True nodes and the same ten UPDATE_OPS, which
slim.learning.create_train_op continues to apply as control dependencies
of the loss.  Only the test/inference graphs change, and they now use the
moving averages that training has been accumulating all along.

Those moving statistics are genuinely trained, so this is fixable at
inference time and does not need retraining.  Reading the decoder
BatchNorm variables straight out of the snapshots shows them leaving their
(mean 0, variance 1) initialisation throughout training: mean
|moving_mean| 0.148 -> 1.198 and mean moving_variance 0.991 -> 3.914
between iteration 250 and iteration 10000.  Note that slim's default decay
of 0.999 makes the estimates lag by roughly a thousand iterations, so a
model stopped after only a few hundred iterations still carries a visible
trace of the initialisation.

This changes results for existing trained models.  Same checkpoint, same
video, batchsize 1: keypoints move by mean 0.81 px, max 2.63 px relative
to the unpatched code on a synthetic 16-frame project, and by mean 2.13 px,
p95 4.56 px, max 7.63 px on a model trained on real frames with stock
augmentation.  That is an order of magnitude larger than the 0.175 px of
batch-composition noise the bug was introducing, so it is a real change and
not a rounding difference.

What justifies accepting it is that the predictions get better, not merely
different.  On a real single-animal clip of 32 frames, where 128
bodypart-frames should each yield one peak, the unpatched network emits 172
detections -- 38 bodypart-frames carrying two or three peaks -- while the
patched one emits 129, of which 127 are exactly right.  It removes 43 of the
44 spurious peaks that would otherwise be fed to the assembler and the
tracker.  Accuracy does not degrade.
Distance from each ground-truth keypoint to the nearest detected peak,
over five snapshots between 250 and 10000 iterations, is equal or slightly
better with the fix; at 10000 iterations it is 3.595 px unpatched at
batchsize 1, 3.655 px unpatched at batchsize 8 and 3.581 px patched at any
batch size.  Predictions become exactly independent of batch composition
(0.00000 px across every comparison above).

Scope: only PoseMultiNet's multi_stage decoder changes behaviour.  In
DeepLabCut 3.0.1 that decoder is reached only when pose_cfg.yaml carries
stride < 8, and no code path writes such a value, so a stock project is
not affected by this change at all.  The other get_net implementations
merely gain the keyword argument so they still satisfy the base-class
signature.

Deliberately not fixed here: PoseMobileNet has the same defect on a larger
scale.  Its extract_features calls mobilenet_v2.training_scope() with no
arguments and that helper defaults to is_training=True, so all 52
MobilenetV2 FusedBatchNormV3 nodes also run in training mode in the
inference graph.  Unlike the decoder that path is reachable from a stock
project (net_type=mobilenet_v2_*), so it deserves its own change and its
own before/after measurements.

Not reported upstream: multi.py carries this arg_scope unchanged on
origin/dev, origin/main, origin/jaap/prepare_tf_deprecation and
origin/backup/tf-deprecation, and no matching issue exists.
---
 deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py       | 8 ++++----
 .../pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/efficientnet.py      | 2 +-
 deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py  | 2 +-
 deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/multi.py      | 6 ++++--
 deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/resnet.py     | 2 +-
 5 files changed, 11 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py
index 1737ba2..5f5fcca 100644
--- a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py
+++ b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py
@@ -27,10 +27,10 @@ class BasePoseNet(metaclass=abc.ABCMeta):
     def extract_features(self, inputs): ...
 
     @abc.abstractmethod
-    def get_net(self, inputs): ...
+    def get_net(self, inputs, is_training=False): ...
 
     def train(self, batch):
-        heads = self.get_net(batch[Batch.inputs])
+        heads = self.get_net(batch[Batch.inputs], is_training=True)
         if self.cfg["weigh_part_predictions"]:
             part_score_weights = batch[Batch.part_score_weights]
         else:
@@ -80,7 +80,7 @@ class BasePoseNet(metaclass=abc.ABCMeta):
         return loss
 
     def test(self, inputs):
-        heads = self.get_net(inputs)
+        heads = self.get_net(inputs, is_training=False)
         return self.add_inference_layers(heads)
 
     def prediction_layers(
@@ -127,7 +127,7 @@ class BasePoseNet(metaclass=abc.ABCMeta):
 
         Added with: https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.11229
         """
-        heads = self.get_net(inputs)
+        heads = self.get_net(inputs, is_training=False)
         locref = heads["locref"]
         probs = tf.sigmoid(heads["part_pred"])
 
diff --git a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/efficientnet.py b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/efficientnet.py
index 3da6d3c..04b8eba 100644
--- a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/efficientnet.py
+++ b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/efficientnet.py
@@ -44,7 +44,7 @@ class PoseEfficientNet(BasePoseNet):
             )
         return net, end_points
 
-    def get_net(self, inputs, use_batch_norm=False, use_drop_out=False):
+    def get_net(self, inputs, use_batch_norm=False, use_drop_out=False, is_training=False):
         net, _ = self.extract_features(inputs, use_batch_norm, use_drop_out)
         return self.prediction_layers(net)
 
diff --git a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py
index 1d4f4ad..ffd34aa 100644
--- a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py
+++ b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py
@@ -93,6 +93,6 @@ class PoseMobileNet(BasePoseNet):
                 )
         return out
 
-    def get_net(self, inputs):
+    def get_net(self, inputs, is_training=False):
         net, end_points = self.extract_features(inputs)
         return self.prediction_layers(net, end_points)
diff --git a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/multi.py b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/multi.py
index 28c08fd..47fca71 100644
--- a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/multi.py
+++ b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/multi.py
@@ -145,6 +145,7 @@ class PoseMultiNet(BasePoseNet):
         input_shape,
         scope="pose",
         reuse=None,
+        is_training=False,
     ):
         net_type = self.cfg["net_type"]
         if self.cfg["multi_stage"]:  # MuNet! (multi_stage decoder + multi_fusion)
@@ -171,6 +172,7 @@ class PoseMultiNet(BasePoseNet):
                 [slim.conv2d],
                 padding="SAME",
                 normalizer_fn=slim.layers.batch_norm,
+                normalizer_params={"is_training": is_training},
                 activation_fn=tf.nn.relu,
                 weights_regularizer=slim.l2_regularizer(self.cfg["weight_decay"]),
             ):
@@ -400,6 +402,6 @@ class PoseMultiNet(BasePoseNet):
         out["features"] = features
         return out
 
-    def get_net(self, inputs):
+    def get_net(self, inputs, is_training=False):
         net, end_points = self.extract_features(inputs)
-        return self.prediction_layers(net, end_points, tf.shape(input=inputs))
+        return self.prediction_layers(net, end_points, tf.shape(input=inputs), is_training=is_training)
diff --git a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/resnet.py b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/resnet.py
index b97bb13..a2a579f 100644
--- a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/resnet.py
+++ b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/resnet.py
@@ -73,6 +73,6 @@ class PoseResnet(BasePoseNet):
                 )
         return out
 
-    def get_net(self, inputs):
+    def get_net(self, inputs, is_training=False):
         net, end_points = self.extract_features(inputs)
         return self.prediction_layers(net, end_points)
-- 
